static constant instead of a function.\n- The double loop for born (checking every neighbor of every live cell) could potentially be more efficient if we pre-compute dead-neighbor counts.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refactoring\n\nMy plan — make these improvements:\n\n1. **Replace `neighborOffsets()` function with a static constant `NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS`** — no need to regenerate the same 8 offsets repeatedly. This reduces invocation count and eliminates an unnecessary function call/array allocation per cell checked.\n\n2. **Pull the neighbor-counting logic into one place** — instead of looping neighbors twice (once for survivors, once for births), compute all needed counts in one pass.\n\nActually, let me think more carefully. Let me go with a cleaner single-pass approach:\n\nInstead of computing survivors separately from births, I'll compute the neighbor count for every cell that's either alive or adjacent to an alive cell, then apply rules from that map. This eliminates the duplication of neighbor iteration.\n\nLet me implement the refactoring"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_86969040b152488ebb39567c","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/game-of-life.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const key = (x: number, y: number): string => `${x},${y}`;\n\n// Yields the 8 neighbor positions of (x, y) as [dx, dy] offsets\nconst neighborOffsets = (): [number, number][] => {\n  const offsets: [number, number][] = [];\n  for (let dx = -1; dx <= 1; dx++) {\n    for (let dy = -1; dy <= 1; dy++) {\n      if (dx !== 0 || dy !== 0) offsets.push([dx, dy]);\n    }\n  }\n  return offsets;\n};\n\nexport const nextGeneration = (cells: [number, number][]): [number, number][] => {\n  const cellSet = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y)));\n\n  const liveNeighborCount = (x: number, y: number): number => {\n    let count = 0;\n    for (const [dx, dy] of neighborOffsets()) {\n      if (cellSet.has(key(x + dx, y + dy))) count++;\n    }\n    return count;\n  };\n\n  const survivors = cells.filter(([x,